+        <p class="summary">A new wearable UV sensor had never been tested in Italy. I compared three of them against the regional environmental agency's reference instrument, recalibrated them, and used them to measure people's real sun exposure.</p>
+        <p class="takeaway"><span class="label">Key findings</span>The factory calibration read about 10% high at midday; after recalibration the sensors agreed with the reference within 10%, but two drifted by −15.8% and −42.6% within three weeks.</p>
+        <details>
+          <summary>Details</summary>
+          <p>The dosimeter was designed by Martin Allen at the University of Canterbury, New Zealand. I did most of the coding (in Maple) and designed the statistical tests. My supervisor provided the instruments and checked the rigor of the work.</p>
+          <ul class="points">
+            <li>The factory calibration overestimated the UV Index by about 10% around midday.</li>
+            <li>I compared a two-factor calibration with a single-factor one. The single-factor fit avoided non-physical UV readings at night, and the recalibrated dosimeters agreed with the reference within 10%.</li>
+            <li>A stability test three weeks later found calibration drift of −15.8% and −42.6% in two dosimeters, which became a key finding for the manufacturer.</li>
+            <li>In field tests, a hiker's dose of 242 J/m² came close to the erythema threshold for their skin type (250 J/m²), matching the mild sunburn they reported.</li>
+          </ul>
+        </details>
